blocked on the mcp adapter rejecting the DPoP authorization scheme: the agent sends `Authorization: DPoP <token>` as RFC 9449 §7.1 requires for a sender-constrained token, but authplanemcp's AuthMiddleware delegates to auth.RequireBearerToken from the MCP go-sdk, which hard-rejects any scheme other than `bearer` and answers 401 "no bearer token" before Authplane's verifyToken ever runs. The example wiring itself is correct (native-first flow, host-visible issuer, AUTHPLANE_DEV_MODE) and the token acquisition leg now succeeds end to end.

Mechanism, verified against go-sdk/mcp v0.2.0 and modelcontextprotocol/go-sdk v1.4.1: auth.verify (auth/auth.go:93) does `strings.ToLower(fields[0]) != "bearer"` and returns 401 immediately. Bumping the MCP go-sdk does not help — v1.6.0 carries the same check. Separately, verifyToken (adapter.go:320) discards the *http.Request, so the proof headers are unreachable and `htu`/`htm`/`ath`/`cnf.jkt` could not be validated even if the scheme check passed. Resource-side DPoP needs adapter support, not a version bump.

This replaces an earlier, now-obsolete reason: the SSRF-pinned POST in go-sdk v0.1.1 stripped the port from the Host header and the client_credentials proof failed the AS `htu` check with invalid_dpop_proof. That is fixed in v0.2.0 — the agent now obtains its DPoP-bound token and fails one step later, at the resource server.

Un-skip and delete this file once authplanemcp accepts the `DPoP` authorization scheme and verifies the proof against the request.
